# Corpus artifacts are digested over their raw committed bytes, so their line
# endings are content rather than presentation. `core.autocrlf` is true on the
# Windows development machine, and without these rules a checkout rewrites the
# generation inputs to CRLF while the Linux verification runner — the platform
# of record for every byte-identity claim (MS-4) — sees LF. The recorded
# `generation_inputs` digests would then fail VR-061 on CI for a line-ending
# reason unrelated to content, which is exactly the trap HINT-004 names.
#
# This materialised during implementation: a stash/pop round-trip rewrote every
# generation input then committed — three at the time, four since FR-037 added
# the manufacturer catalogue. Pinning them here makes the working copy agree with
# the index on every platform instead of relying on nobody running the wrong
# git command.
data/corpus/**/*.json text eol=lf
data/corpus/**/*.md   text eol=lf

# Vendored specifications and generated documents are binary. Git already
# detects them, but saying so explicitly keeps a future `text=auto` from ever
# reaching them — a single rewritten byte breaks `content_hash`.
data/corpus/**/*.pdf  binary

# The roster fixture is hashed over a canonical re-serialization rather than
# raw bytes, so its digest already survives a line-ending rewrite by design
# (E001 VR-008b). It is pinned anyway: the file is compared byte-for-byte by
# other checks, and one convention for `data/` is easier to keep than two.
data/roster/**/*.json text eol=lf
data/roster/**/*.md   text eol=lf

# E005's emitted artifacts. Like the roster, the dataset fixture is hashed over
# a canonical re-serialization of its *parsed* content, so its digest survives a
# line-ending rewrite by design (AD-001) — that property is the mechanism, and
# pinning the file does not weaken it. Pinned anyway for the same two reasons:
# the fixture and the ground-truth record are compared byte-for-byte by the
# deterministic-ordering check (DV-023, two runs at one seed emit byte-identical
# payloads), and one convention for everything under `data/` is easier to keep
# than three. The datasheet is Markdown and is emitted by the same run.
data/procurement/**/*.json   text eol=lf
data/procurement/**/*.md     text eol=lf
data/ground-truth/**/*.json  text eol=lf

# The encoder artifact is digest-verified before a session is created, so its
# committed bytes are content. Without these rules `core.autocrlf` smudges the
# JSON on a Windows checkout and the digests fail there while passing on Linux
# — or the reverse, which is what happened: two records were written from a
# CRLF working tree and the LF blobs then failed on the Linux runner.
data/encoder/**/*.json text eol=lf
data/encoder/**/*.txt  text eol=lf
data/encoder/**/*.md   text eol=lf
data/encoder/*.onnx    binary
